The Story Behind the
Estate Sale House =E2=80=94 in four parts

Part Three

"Siegfried"

In July 1989=2C Siegfried E. Brockmann (1928=E2=80=932025) was called into=
 the office of Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s lawyer. He learned that he had been beq=
ueathed Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s house=2C the Maybeck Studio at 2711 Buena Vist=
a Way. The gift was not entirely unexpected=2C but Siegfried was overcome=
 by Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s generosity and realized that it would change the c=
ourse of his life.

Siegfried had been born in D=C3=B6beln=2C a small city in Saxony near Dres=
den. His idyllic childhood=2C surrounded by family and friends=2C changed=
 in the early 1930s when a new government regime came into being which bro=
ught with it a pall that hung over everyday existence. When Siegfried=2C a=
t age 15=2C was in high school=2C he=2C along with his fellow students=2C=
 was  sent to the front to serve as a Luftwaffenhelfer (the wartime job mo=
st of the youngsters were given). In the spring of 1945=2C when the end se=
emed near=2C the officer for Siegfried=E2=80=99s unit told all the schoolb=
oys to head for home. He did not want them to be taken prisoner by the Rus=
sians.

Siegfried with his two older sisters and mother in D=C3=B6beln about 1935.

At War=E2=80=99s end=2C Siegfried=2C and all the others at home=2C hoped a=
nd prayed that the Americans would take control. Instead=2C their quadrant=
 of Germany was turned over to Russia. Life was even worse under this fore=
ign regime=2C but Siegfried learned that a pharmaceutical company was abou=
t to open and he and two school friends applied as interns. After two year=
s=2C he received his certificate and decided that the time to escape was n=
ow! A friend gave him detailed instructions on how to reach the border and=
 how to cross the border; he even gave Siegfried contacts in both Goslar a=
nd Munich.

Siegfried=2C on the left=2C in D=C3=B6blen with two school friends=2C Irmg=
ard and Werner=2C shortly before his escape in 1948.

In the West=2C Siegfried discovered that the chemistry certificate meant n=
othing with out a residence permit. After about a week of seeking employme=
nt=2C he was resigned to having to return to the Russian Zone. But before=
 he left Munich=2C Siegfried received assistance through a chance encounte=
r and was directed to a Press Control Officer working for the U.S. Militar=
y Government. This was Walter Brockmann=2C who told Siegfried to come back=
 the next day and he might have an answer. Walter was told by the other go=
vernment officials that the only way to keep someone from having to return=
 to the East was if an American=E2=80=94Walter Brockmann in this case=E2=
=80=94were to adopt him. Walter was intrigued=2C and started the proceedin=
gs with help from influential friends living on nearby Lake Starnberg who=
 wrote letters of recommendation and also contacted Siegfried=E2=80=99s mo=
ther. After the adoption took place=2C the Starnberg friends became Siegfr=
ied=E2=80=99s honorary godparents.

The sought-after Identification Card.

Walter felt ready to retire from the military=2C and off they went to New=
 York City on the USNS General Alexander M. Patch where Siegfried had his=
 first Thanksgiving dinner on board. Upon arrival=2C Walter sold his colle=
ction of D=C3=BCrer engravings so that Siegfried could finish high school=
=2C and sent him to Suffield Academy in Connecticut. The instructors were=
 impressed with Siegfried=E2=80=99s knowledge of chemistry and recommend h=
e apply to American University=2C which he did.

In December 1949=2C after the fall semester had ended=2C Siegfried ran int=
o a classmate in downtown Washington=2C D.C. who told him that a friend wa=
s giving him a farewell party and that he could bring a guest. On New Year=
=E2=80=99s Day 1950=2C Siegfried took the long streetcar ride to Chevy Cha=
se. At the gathering he met the girl who was giving the party=2C Maga van=
 Barneveld Cole=2C and her grandmother=2C her parents=2C and her uncle=2C=
 James Cole=2C a chemistry professor at the University of Virginia. Other=
 visits at the house on Quesada Street in Chevy Chase followed. The fun-lo=
ving Maga and Siegfried soon became close friends=2C and Siegfried became=
 the son that the Coles never had=2C and they=2C in turn=2C were Siegfried=
=E2=80=99s American family.

Maga Cole and Siegfried in Maga's Ford. Water Brockmann=2C 1951

Prof. Cole arranged for Siegfried to attend the University of Virginia whi=
le helping him with research. While there=2C Siegfried often spent weekend=
s in Washington with Walter and the Coles. And sometimes the Coles would p=
ick up Siegfried on a Friday and take him to Palm Beach. Mrs. van Barnevel=
d wrote of such an impromptu trip=2C =E2=80=9CMaga and Siegfried run so be=
autifully to the water=E2=80=99s edge. Siegfried=E2=80=99s first touch of=
 ocean.=E2=80=9D

Siegfried reading over the University of Virginia catalog with Mrs. Cole.=
 Walter Brockmann=2C 1950

After graduation in 1954=2C Siegfried received a teaching fellowship at Ha=
rvard. Eventually he realized that the academic life was not for him. When=
 Cole Chemical Company in St. Louis offered him a position=2C he gladly ac=
cepted. Siegfried had worked summers at the company=2C staying with the Co=
les. Now he was a permanent part of the company=2C and a permanent St. Lou=
is resident. Mrs. Cole found an apartment for Siegfried which she furnishe=
d. The sofa for sale this weekend was custom-made by Mrs. Cole for the apa=
rtment. Siegfried tried his hand at furniture design: two angular marble-t=
opped tables constructed by the Cole Company workshop.

A few years later=2C Siegfried was able to buy a house in the Central West=
 End. From a weed patch in the back of his new house=2C Siegfried created=
 one of the world's most charming gardens. He discovered that he had moved=
 into a neighborhood of interesting people. Two doors way lived Marie Tayl=
or=2C an artist whose work will be at the Sale. Next door were members of=
 one of St. Louis's earliest families. Through them=2C he was introduced t=
o many who became lifelong friends=2C as well as to a type of social life=
 he had never before known.

Siegfried and his St. Louis garden.

Maga Cole left for Berkeley in 1958 to continue her study of architecture.=
 She rented the cottage=2C Arrilaga=2C from the Maybecks=2C where Siegfrie=
d stayed on his many trips to Berkeley. On the first visit=2C in 1959=2C M=
aga introduced him to the Maybeck children: Kerna and Wallen. As a guest a=
t Wallen and Jacomena=E2=80=99s house=2C Siegfried was served his first Ma=
nhattan.

During the next thirty years=2C especially after Mrs. Cole had also moved=
 to Berkeley=2C Siegfried and Walter made a semi-annual pilgrimage to =E2=
=80=9CMaybeck Country=2C=E2=80=9D often staying at the French Hotel. It wa=
s on such a visit in July 1989 (five years after Mrs. Cole had lost her da=
ughter Maga)=2C that Siegfried=2C arriving at Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s home at=
 the appointed time the next morning=2C was met at the front door by her l=
awyer and executor.

With the gift of Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s very special Maybeck house=2C which w=
as filled with happy memories=2C Siegfried made the decision to retire ear=
ly from the pharmaceutical company and move to Berkeley. Walter was elderl=
y and needed his care=2C which was a main factor in the decision to retire=
=2E The moving van (carrying belongings from Siegfried=E2=80=99s three-story=
 St. Louis house) arrived at 2711 Buena Vista Way on October 31=2C 1990. T=
he date happened to be Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s birthday.

Under the friendly wing of Jacomena Maybeck=2C Siegfried quickly acclimati=
zed to his new surroundings=2C making friends with all the neighbors=2C an=
d going to Edy=E2=80=99s every day for lunch (a favorite spot that Walter=
 and Siegfried had discovered on earlier visits). When Jacomena and I appr=
oached them about including the Studio on the upcoming 1992 BAHA house tou=
r=2C they were most agreeable. During the tour preparations I made several=
 visits to the Studio=2C and we soon became friends. Walter died later tha=
t year. Siegfried continued in the house that was now almost a living memo=
rial to two of the most important people in his life. Siegfried said there=
 was not one day that he did not sit in the living room and think of Walte=
r and Mrs. Cole.

Siegfried and Walter at the 1992 BAHA house tour.

Siegfried was proud to be the steward of a Maybeck house. He was eager to=
 share it with others. A request from an out-of-state visitor to see a May=
beck house (usually made through the BAHA office!)=2C or a group of archit=
ects from Japan or Holland who wanted to see the =E2=80=9CSack House=E2=80=
=9D=E2=80=94 all were welcomed. Siegfried opened his home for many BAHA ev=
ents over the years; the last time being the afternoon that Maybeck=E2=80=
=99s Packard made an appearance on Buena Vista Way one summer afternoon in=
 2016.

Siegfried=2C on the left=2C with guests at the BAHA "Maybeck Country" tour=
=2E Daniella Thompson=2C 2009

As did Mrs. Cole before him=2C Siegfried made improvements to the property=
=2E He created a terrace in front of the house=2C using the same type of agg=
regate paving that the Maybecks had used elsewhere in the garden. He added=
 counters and cupboards to the bare walls of the tiny kitchen=2C and put i=
n central heating=2C which=2C along with Mrs. Cole=E2=80=99s heavy =E2=80=
=9CMaybeck=E2=80=9D draperies=2C assured that the Studio was warm and welc=
oming in winter. Siegfried enjoyed working in the garden and relaxing on t=
he terrace as the sun set over the bay in the distance.

Siegfried died on December 27=2C 2025=2C his 97th birthday. The Siegfried=
 E. Brockmann Trust is pleased that BAHA members will  enjoy the Maybeck S=
tudio this weekend. Soon=2C  a new chapter in the history of the house wil=
l begin.

=E2=80=94Anthony Bruce 2026

VOLUNTEERS WANTED

Volunteers are needed to help in different capacities during  the two days=
 of the Sale. Most of Saturday is covered; there are still quite a few shi=
fts open for Sunday

Volunteers receive free admission to the event.

