p;the<br>
Estate Sale House &mdash; in four parts</span></em></strong></span></span>=
<br>
<br>
<span style=3D"color:#ff0066"><span style=3D"font-size:24px"><font face=3D=
"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif"><span style=3D"caret-color:=
rgb(75=2C 0=2C 130)"><strong>Part Three</strong></span></font></span><br>
<br>
<span style=3D"font-size:30px"><font face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new=
 roman=2C serif"><span style=3D"caret-color:rgb(75=2C 0=2C 130)"><strong>&=
quot;Siegfried&quot;</strong></span></font></span></span></p>

<div style=3D"text-align: left;color: #505050;font-family: Arial;font-size=
: 14px;line-height: 150%;">
<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">In July 198=
9=2C Siegfried E. Brockmann (1928&ndash;2025) was called into the office o=
f Mrs. Cole&rsquo;s lawyer. He learned that he had been bequeathed Mrs. Co=
le&rsquo;s house=2C the Maybeck Studio at 2711 Buena Vista Way. The gift w=
as not entirely unexpected=2C but Siegfried was overcome by Mrs. Cole&rsqu=
o;s generosity and realized that it would change the course of his life.</=
span></span></span></p>

<p><font color=3D"#000000" face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C=
 serif" size=3D"3">Siegfried had been born in D&ouml;beln=2C a small city=
 in Saxony near Dresden. His idyllic childhood=2C surrounded by family and=
 friends=2C changed in the early 1930s when a new government regime came i=
nto being which brought with it a pall that hung over everyday existence.=
 When Siegfried=2C at age 15=2C was in high school=2C he=2C along with his=
 fellow students=2C was&nbsp;&nbsp;sent to the front to serve as a </font>=
<em style=3D"color:rgb(0=2C 0=2C 0); font-family:georgia=2C times=2C &quot=
;times new roman=2C serif; font-size:16px">Luftwaffenhelfer&nbsp;</em><spa=
n style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"fo=
nt-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">(the wartime job m=
ost of the youngsters were given)</span></span></span><font color=3D"#0000=
00" face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif" size=3D"3">. In=
 the spring of 1945=2C when the end seemed near=2C the officer for Siegfri=
ed&rsquo;s unit&nbsp;told all the schoolboys to head for home. He did not=
 want them to be taken&nbsp;<span style=3D"caret-color:rgb(0=2C 0=2C 0)">p=
risoner</span>&nbsp;by the Russians.</font></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725205" height=3D"1=
70" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/7d48=
e92a-6984-9840-91d1-b759da7e22ef.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 170px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Siegfried with his two older sisters and mother in D&ouml;beln about 1935.=
</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">At War&rsqu=
o;s end=2C Siegfried=2C and all the others at home=2C hoped and prayed tha=
t the Americans would take control. Instead=2C their quadrant of Germany w=
as turned over to Russia. Life was even worse under this foreign regime=2C=
 but Siegfried learned that a pharmaceutical company was about to open and=
 he and two school friends applied as interns. After two years=2C he recei=
ved his certificate and decided that the time to escape was now! A friend=
 gave him detailed instructions on how to reach the border and&nbsp;how to=
 cross the border; he even gave Siegfried contacts in both Goslar and Muni=
ch.</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725206" height=3D"2=
36" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/a942=
ec3c-6ce7-5afb-482c-bd31119fc41d.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 236px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Siegfried=2C on the left=2C in D&ouml;blen with two school friends=2C Irmg=
ard and Werner=2C shortly before his escape in 1948.</p>

<p><font color=3D"#000000" face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C=
 serif" size=3D"3">In the West=2C Siegfried discovered that the chemistry=
 certificate meant nothing with out a residence permit. After about a week=
 of seeking employment=2C he was resigned to having to return to the Russi=
an Zone. But before he left Munich=2C Siegfried received assistance throug=
h a chance encounter and was directed to a Press Control Officer working f=
or the U.S. Military Government. This was Walter Brockmann=2C who told Sie=
gfried to come back the next day and he might have an answer. Walter was t=
old by the other government officials that the only way to keep someone fr=
om having to return to the East&nbsp;was if an American&mdash;Walter Brock=
mann in this case&mdash;were to adopt him. Walter was intrigued=2C and sta=
rted the proceedings with help from influential friends living on&nbsp;<sp=
an style=3D"caret-color:rgb(0=2C 0=2C 0)">nearby</span>&nbsp;Lake Starnber=
g who wrote letters of recommendation and also contacted Siegfried&rsquo;s=
 mother. After the adoption took place=2C the Starnberg friends became Sie=
gfried&rsquo;s honorary godparents.&nbsp;</font></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725207" height=3D"2=
73" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/1a2c=
77c7-2c64-2f70-478a-d3b86cf9421d.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 273px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
The sought-after Identification Card.</p>

<p><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C ti=
mes=2C times new roman=2C serif"><span style=3D"color:#000000">Walter felt=
 ready to retire from the military=2C and off they went to New York City o=
n</span><span style=3D"color:#000000"> the USNS General Alexander M. Patch=
 </span><span style=3D"color:#000000">where Siegfried had his first Thanks=
giving dinner on board. Upon arrival=2C </span></span></span><span style=
=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times n=
ew roman=2C serif"><span style=3D"color:#000000">Walter sold his collectio=
n of D&uuml;rer engravings s</span></span></span><span style=3D"font-size:=
16px"><span style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C se=
rif"><span style=3D"color:#000000">o that Siegfried could finish high scho=
ol=2C and sent him to Suffield Academy in Connecticut. The instructors wer=
e impressed with Siegfried&rsquo;s knowledge of chemistry and recommend he=
 apply to American University=2C which he did.</span></span></span></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">In December=
 1949=2C after the fall semester had ended=2C Siegfried ran into a classma=
te in downtown Washington=2C D.C. who told him that a friend was giving hi=
m a farewell party and that he could bring a guest. On New Year&rsquo;s Da=
y 1950=2C Siegfried took the long streetcar ride to Chevy Chase. At the ga=
thering he met the girl who was giving the party=2C Maga van Barneveld Col=
e=2C and her grandmother=2C her parents=2C and her uncle=2C James Cole=2C=
 a chemistry professor at the University of Virginia. Other visits at the=
 house on Quesada Street in Chevy Chase followed. The fun-loving Maga and=
 Siegfried soon became close friends=2C and Siegfried became the son that=
 the Coles never had=2C and they=2C in turn=2C were Siegfried&rsquo;s Amer=
ican family.</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725204" height=3D"2=
42" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/8b9c=
c66d-bd3a-dd09-3a74-f8b7ca876047.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 242px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;">Maga Cole and Siegfried in Maga&#39;s For=
d. Water Brockmann=2C 1951</p>

<p style=3D"text-align: left;"><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=
=3D"font-size:16px"><span style=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times n=
ew roman=2C serif">Prof. Cole arranged for Siegfried to attend the Univers=
ity of Virginia while helping him with research. While there=2C Siegfried=
 often spent weekends in Washington with Walter and the Coles. And sometim=
es the Coles would pick up Siegfried on a&nbsp;Friday and take him to Palm=
 Beach. Mrs. van Barneveld wrote of such an impromptu trip=2C &ldquo;Maga=
 and Siegfried run so beautifully to the water&rsquo;s edge. Siegfried&rsq=
uo;s first touch of ocean.&rdquo;</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><br>
<img data-file-id=3D"6725228" height=3D"300" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.=
com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/4e5d4507-d5d3-221a-a2cb-221246249221.=
gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;height: 300px;margin: 0px;line-heig=
ht: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: none;display: inline;max-width: 46=
0px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Siegfried reading over the University of Virginia catalog with Mrs. Cole.=
 Walter Brockmann=2C 1950</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">After gradu=
ation in 1954=2C Siegfried received a teaching fellowship at Harvard. Even=
tually he realized that the academic life was not for him. When Cole Chemi=
cal Company in St. Louis offered him a position=2C he gladly accepted. Sie=
gfried had worked summers at the company=2C staying with the Coles. Now he=
 was a permanent part of the company=2C and a permanent St. Louis resident=
=2E Mrs. Cole found an apartment for Siegfried which she furnished. The sofa=
 for sale this weekend was custom-made by Mrs. Cole for the apartment. Sie=
gfried tried his hand at furniture design: two angular marble-topped table=
s constructed by the Cole Company workshop.</span></span></span><br>
<br>
<font color=3D"#000000" face=3D"georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C ser=
if" size=3D"3">A few years later=2C Siegfried was able to buy a house in t=
he Central West End. From a weed patch in the back of his new house=2C Sie=
gfried created one of the world&#39;s most charming gardens. He discovered=
 that he had moved into a neighborhood of interesting people. Two doors wa=
y lived Marie Taylor=2C an artist whose work will be at the Sale. Next doo=
r were members of one&nbsp;<span style=3D"caret-color:rgb(0=2C 0=2C 0)">of=
</span>&nbsp;St. Louis&#39;s earliest families. Through them=2C he was&nbs=
p;<span style=3D"caret-color:rgb(0=2C 0=2C 0)">introduced to many who beca=
me lifelong friends=2C as well as to a type of social life he had never be=
fore known.&nbsp;</span></font></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725226" height=3D"3=
50" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/1348=
9dc3-4d5f-f688-6e51-71f3f5871e74.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 350px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Siegfried and his St. Louis garden.</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Maga Cole l=
eft for Berkeley in 1958 to continue her study of architecture. She rented=
 the cottage=2C Arrilaga=2C from the Maybecks=2C where Siegfried stayed on=
 his many trips to Berkeley. On the first visit=2C in 1959=2C Maga introdu=
ced him to the Maybeck children: Kerna and Wallen. As a guest at Wallen an=
d Jacomena&rsquo;s house=2C Siegfried was served his first Manhattan. &nbs=
p;</span></span></span></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">During the=
 next thirty years=2C especially after Mrs. Cole had also moved to Berkele=
y=2C Siegfried and Walter made a semi-annual pilgrimage to &ldquo;Maybeck=
 Country=2C&rdquo; often staying at the French Hotel. It was on such a vis=
it in July 1989 (five years after Mrs. Cole had lost her daughter Maga)=2C=
 that Siegfried=2C arriving at Mrs. Cole&rsquo;s home at the appointed tim=
e the next morning=2C was met at the front door by her lawyer and executor=
=2E</span></span></span></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">With the gi=
ft of Mrs. Cole&rsquo;s very special Maybeck house=2C which was filled wit=
h happy memories=2C Siegfried made the decision to retire early from the p=
harmaceutical company and move to Berkeley. Walter was elderly and needed=
 his care=2C which was a main factor in the decision to retire. The moving=
 van (carrying belongings from Siegfried&rsquo;s three-story St. Louis hou=
se) arrived at 2711 Buena Vista Way on October 31=2C 1990. The date happen=
ed to be Mrs. Cole&rsquo;s birthday.</span></span></span></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Under the f=
riendly wing of Jacomena Maybeck=2C Siegfried quickly acclimatized to his=
 new surroundings=2C making friends with all the neighbors=2C and going to=
 Edy&rsquo;s every day for lunch (a favorite spot that Walter and Siegfrie=
d had discovered on earlier visits). When Jacomena and I approached them a=
bout including the Studio on the upcoming 1992 BAHA house tour=2C they wer=
e most agreeable. During the tour preparations I made several visits to th=
e Studio=2C and we soon became friends. Walter died later that year. Siegf=
ried continued in the house that was now almost a living memorial to two o=
f the most important people in his life. Siegfried said there was not one=
 day that he did not sit in the living room and think of Walter and Mrs. C=
ole.</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725227" height=3D"3=
09" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/49e6=
83fe-1398-8dde-ddcf-2227c9a2479b.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 309px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Siegfried and Walter at the 1992 BAHA house tour.</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Siegfried w=
as proud to be the steward of a Maybeck house. He was eager to share it wi=
th others. A request from an out-of-state visitor to see a Maybeck house (=
usually made through the BAHA office!)=2C or a group of architects from Ja=
pan or Holland who wanted to see the &ldquo;Sack House&rdquo;&mdash; all w=
ere welcomed. Siegfried opened his home for many BAHA events over the year=
s; the last time being the afternoon that Maybeck&rsquo;s Packard made an=
 appearance on Buena Vista Way one summer afternoon in 2016.</span></span>=
</span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725229" height=3D"2=
76" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/cf2b=
f700-98d0-907b-a732-62ff3566b35a.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 276px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"><br>
Siegfried=2C on the left=2C with guests at the BAHA &quot;Maybeck Country&=
quot; tour. Daniella Thompson=2C 2009</p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">As did Mrs.=
 Cole before him=2C Siegfried made improvements to the property. He create=
d a terrace in front of the house=2C using the same type of aggregate pavi=
ng that the Maybecks had used elsewhere in the garden. He added counters a=
nd cupboards to the bare walls of the tiny kitchen=2C and put in central h=
eating=2C which=2C along with Mrs. Cole&rsquo;s heavy &ldquo;Maybeck&rdquo=
; draperies=2C assured that the Studio was warm and welcoming in winter. S=
iegfried enjoyed working in the garden and relaxing on the terrace as the=
 sun set over the bay in the distance.</span></span></span></p>

<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><img data-file-id=3D"6725230" height=3D"3=
73" src=3D"https://mcusercontent.com/cf07d73ef679275ea4445c763/images/9517=
a714-3da4-a238-2eff-607b1260564a.gif" style=3D"border: 0px;width: 432px;he=
ight: 373px;margin: 0px;line-height: 100%;outline: none;text-decoration: n=
one;display: inline;max-width: 460px;" width=3D"432"></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">Siegfried d=
ied on December 27=2C 2025=2C his 97th birthday. The Siegfried E. Brockman=
n Trust is pleased that BAHA members will&nbsp; enjoy the Maybeck Studio t=
his weekend. Soon=2C &nbsp;a new chapter in the history of the house will=
 begin.</span></span></span></p>

<p><span style=3D"color:#000000"><span style=3D"font-size:16px"><span styl=
e=3D"font-family:georgia=2C times=2C times new roman=2C serif">&mdash;Anth=
